      "text": "��<!DOCTYPE html PUBLIC \"-//W3C//DTD XHTML 1.0 Transitional//EN\" \"http://www.w3.org/TR/xhtml1/DTD/xhtml1-transitional.dtd\"><html><body><p><p>In 1920, Watson conducted an interesting experiment with a little boy named Albert, a white rat, and a loud noise.�</p>\n<div>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><img width=\"130\" height=\"106\" src=\"http://mindhacks-legacy.s3.amazonaws.com/2009/10/little-albert.jpg\"></p>\n<p>Albert was a little boy who had no fear of most things in his environment. However, people have some instinctive or natural fears. One of these is a fear of loud noises because they are startling and they are usually associated with danger.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><img width=\"143\" height=\"140\" src=\"https://encrypted-tbn1.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcS930q5P3GdZRj5ApWGlXixNQzWzK442YZgmMBy4rflFOFXTXHOyA\"></p>\n<p>One day Albert was introduced to a white rat and a loud noise was made at the same time. Albert was startled and began to cry.�</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><a id=\"irc_mil\" href=\"https://www.google.ca/url?sa=i&rct=j&q=&esrc=s&source=images&cd=&cad=rja&uact=8&ved=0ahUKEwjUz4f8g9LTAhWZ8oMKHXfABaIQjRwIBw&url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.simplypsychology.org%2Fclassical-conditioning.html&psig=AFQjCNGuETLSg4cZbTPwjVvO-vtAqcE4xA&ust=1493842646394642\" data-ved=\"0ahUKEwjUz4f8g9LTAhWZ8oMKHXfABaIQjRwIBw\" jsaction=\"mousedown:irc.rl;keydown:irc.rlk;irc.il;\"><img width=\"356\" height=\"248\" id=\"irc_mi\" alt=\"Image result for albert and the white rat\" src=\"https://www.simplypsychology.org/little-albert.jpg\"></a><a id=\"irc_mil\" href=\"https://www.google.ca/url?sa=i&rct=j&q=&esrc=s&source=images&cd=&cad=rja&uact=8&ved=0ahUKEwiV5Onqg9LTAhXHy4MKHTzsAOYQjRwIBw&url=https%3A%2F%2Fstephaniedelaosa.wordpress.com%2F2013%2F10%2F11%2Fexperiment-little-albert%2F&psig=AFQjCNGuETLSg4cZbTPwjVvO-vtAqcE4xA&ust=1493842646394642\" data-ved=\"0ahUKEwiV5Onqg9LTAhXHy4MKHTzsAOYQjRwIBw\" jsaction=\"mousedown:irc.rl;keydown:irc.rlk;irc.il;\"></a></p>\n<p>From that time on, a loud noise was made every time a rat appeared. Soon, Albert feared the white rat, even when he saw it without a loud noise. Alberta had been conditioned to fear the white rat.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><img src=\"https://stephaniedelaosa.files.wordpress.com/2013/10/baba.jpg\" alt=\"Image result for albert and the white rat\" width=\"400\" height=\"169\" class=\"img-responsive atto_image_button_text-bottom\"></p>\n<p>Besides his work with Albert, Watson was involved in other experimental studies with children. Watson was soon regarded as an expert on child rearing and child care. His advice was sought by many parents. Because of his Behaviourist philosophy on programming behavior, he was rather strict and disciplined views on child care.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n</div></p></body></html>",
